Production-Grade Python Pipelines
Real-Time Capital Gain Processing System (BSE)

Business Scenario
Imagine you are developing a Production-Grade Capital Gain Processing System for the Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE). Every trading day, millions of transactions are executed across multiple exchanges and brokers. At the end of the trading day, these transactions must be processed to accurately calculate investors' capital gains for taxation and reporting purposes.
The system is responsible for processing data received from multiple sources every night and generating accurate tax reports before the next business day.
Data Sources
The Capital Gain Processing System receives data from multiple upstream systems, including:
•	Trading Engine 
•	Clearing Corporation 
•	Depository (CDSL / NSDL) 
•	Corporate Actions Team 
•	Mutual Fund Platforms 
•	Bonus / Split Processing Systems
Daily Processing Volume
A production-grade Capital Gain Processing System is designed to handle extremely large datasets.
Typical daily workload includes:
•	40–50 Million Trade Records 
•	5–10 GB Trade Files 
•	Thousands of Securities 
•	Millions of Investor Accounts 
The entire processing pipeline must complete successfully within the available overnight processing window.
Business Objective
The primary objective of the system is to generate accurate and compliant outputs, including:
•	FIFO Holdings 
•	Short-Term Capital Gain (STCG) 
•	Long-Term Capital Gain (LTCG) 
•	Tax Reports 
•	Investor Statements 
The processing must be reliable, scalable, fault tolerant, and capable of completing without failure.
Production Pipeline Overview
Trade Files
      │
      ▼
Validation
      │
      ▼
Corporate Action Adjustment
      │
      ▼
FIFO Matching
      │
      ▼
Capital Gain Calculation
      │
      ▼
Tax Classification
      │
      ▼
Database Loading
      │
      ▼
Investor Reports
Each processing stage performs a specific business function and operates independently, enabling easier maintenance, testing, and scalability.
1. Modular Pipeline Architecture
Business Problem
Many beginners develop data processing applications by placing the entire logic inside a single Python file.
read_file()

clean_data()

calculate_fifo()

calculate_tax()

save_to_database()
While this approach may work for small datasets (for example, 100 records), it becomes difficult to maintain and scale when processing 5 crore (50 million) trade records.
Production Architecture
Enterprise systems divide responsibilities into separate modules.
capital_gain_pipeline/

│
├── config/
│      config.yaml
│
├── ingestion/
│      trade_loader.py
│      holdings_loader.py
│      corporate_action_loader.py
│
├── validation/
│      validator.py
│
├── transformation/
│      fifo_engine.py
│      adjustment_engine.py
│
├── taxation/
│      capital_gain.py
│
├── loading/
│      mysql_loader.py
│
├── logger/
│      logger.py
│
├── retry/
│      retry.py
│
└── main.py
Each module is responsible for a single business function.
Real-Time BSE Processing Flow
Trade File
      │
      ▼
Validation
      │
      ▼
Corporate Action Adjustment
      │
      ▼
FIFO Matching
      │
      ▼
Capital Gain Calculation
      │
      ▼
Tax Classification
      │
      ▼
Database
      │
      ▼
Reports
Every block operates independently.
For example, if tax regulations change in the future, only the taxation module requires modification.
taxation/
    capital_gain.py
The remaining application continues to function without changes.
Why Modular Architecture?
Consider a future scenario where Budget 2027 revises the Long-Term Capital Gain (LTCG) tax rate from 12.5% to 15%.
Without modular architecture:
•	Multiple files require modification. 
•	Regression risk increases. 
•	Testing effort becomes extensive. 
With modular architecture:
Only
taxation/capital_gain.py
requires updates.
The remainder of the application remains unchanged.
This is how enterprise-scale financial systems are designed and maintained.
2. Configuration-Driven Ingestion
Business Problem
BSE receives trade files from multiple brokerage firms.
Examples include:
•	Zerodha 
•	Upstox 
•	ICICI Direct 
•	HDFC Securities 
•	Kotak Securities 
Each broker follows its own file format.
Example
Zerodha
TradeDate
ISIN
Qty
Price
ICICI
Date
SecurityCode
Quantity
Rate
Modifying application code for every new broker is neither scalable nor maintainable.
Production Solution
A centralized configuration file maps broker-specific column names.
config.yaml

zerodha:

 trade_date: TradeDate

 quantity: Qty

 price: Price

 isin: ISIN

icici:

 trade_date: Date

 quantity: Quantity

 price: Rate

 isin: SecurityCode
The ingestion engine reads the configuration and automatically maps the incoming file structure.
Benefits
When a new broker joins the exchange:
No application code changes are required.
Only the configuration file is updated.
For example:
angel:

 quantity: QTY

 price: TRADE_PRICE
The pipeline immediately supports the new broker format without requiring deployment or code modification.
This is known as Configuration-Driven Design, a common practice in enterprise financial systems.
3. Parallel Ingestion Using Multiprocessing
Business Scenario
Daily files received by BSE may include:
•	Trade File – 15 GB 
•	Corporate Action File – 2 GB 
•	Dividend File – 1 GB 
•	Mutual Fund File – 4 GB 
•	Broker Files – 20 GB 
Sequential Processing
Trade
      │
Corporate
      │
Dividend
      │
Mutual Fund
      │
Broker
Approximate processing time:
40 Minutes
Parallel Processing
Modern production systems distribute processing across multiple CPU cores.
CPU 1 → Trade

CPU 2 → Corporate

CPU 3 → Dividend

CPU 4 → Mutual Fund

CPU 5 → Broker
All files are processed simultaneously.
Approximate processing time:
40 Minutes → 9 Minutes
Real Production Example
Pool(8)

↓

8 CPU Cores

↓

8 Parallel Loaders
Parallel processing significantly improves throughput and is widely used across stock exchanges, banks, and financial institutions.
4. Error Handling and Retry Mechanism
Business Scenario
While downloading today's trade file, the network connection is temporarily interrupted.
Instead of terminating the application immediately, production systems automatically retry the failed operation.
Attempt 1

Failed

↓

Attempt 2

Failed

↓

Attempt 3

Success
The failure is recovered without manual intervention.

Database Connectivity Scenario
Temporary database outages should not terminate the entire overnight processing pipeline.
Instead, the application performs retries using Exponential Backoff.
Retry after

5 Seconds

↓

10 Seconds

↓

20 Seconds
Real Banking Example
MySQL Connection Lost

↓

Retry 5 Times

↓

Reconnect

↓

Continue Processing
Without an automated retry mechanism, an entire night's processing could fail because of a short-lived network interruption.
5. Unit Testing Ingestion Logic
Production systems validate business rules before processing begins.
Example:
assert validate_quantity(100) => pass

assert  validate_quantity(-100) => fail

assert  not  validate_quantity(-100) => passes

Negative quantities are rejected.
Another validation example:
Trade Date

2026-15-99	
Invalid date.
The record is rejected before entering the processing pipeline.
Real BSE Validation Scenarios
Production systems execute thousands of automated validation checks.
Examples include:
•	Duplicate Trade ID 
•	Negative Price 
•	Quantity Zero 
•	Invalid ISIN 
•	Invalid Settlement Date 
•	Future Trade Date 
•	Missing PAN 
•	Invalid Investor ID 
Example
Input
Trade ID

1001

1001

1002
Expected Result
Duplicate Trade Detected

Second Trade Rejected
Incorrect capital gain calculations are prevented before processing begins.
6. Coding Standards & Deployment Considerations
Enterprise applications are evaluated not only on correctness but also on maintainability, readability, and operational support.
Calculator.py
  Calc_addition(num1,num2):
   Result_of_addition = num1  + num2
Poor Coding Practice
a = 10

b = 20

c = a + b
Production Coding Practice
purchase_price = 1000

sale_price = 1200

capital_gain = sale_price - purchase_price
Business intent becomes immediately understandable.

Logging Standards
Avoid using:
print("Done")
Instead:
logger.info("Trade file processed successfully")
Example Log Entry
2026-07-31 22:15:10

INFO

Trade File Loaded

Records

15,623,812
Example Error Log
ERROR

Trade ID

45872191

Reason

Invalid ISIN
Comprehensive logging enables Operations and Support teams to quickly investigate production issues.
Deployment Considerations
Every night, settlement files are received from the exchange.
A scheduler such as Cron, Apache Airflow, or an enterprise scheduling solution automatically triggers the processing pipeline.
11:30 PM

↓

Read Trade Files

↓

Validate

↓

FIFO

↓

Capital Gain

↓

Tax

↓

Database

↓

Generate Reports

↓

Email Completion Status

↓

Archive Files
If any processing stage fails, the Operations Team receives an alert containing the failed step and the associated error details.

End-to-End Production Workflow
                     Broker Trade Files
                             │
                             ▼
              Configuration-Based Loader
                             │
                             ▼
              Schema & Business Validation
                             │
                             ▼
          Parallel Ingestion (Multiple CPUs)
                             │
                             ▼
        Corporate Action Adjustments
       (Bonus, Split, Merger, Dividend)
                             │
                             ▼
