                             │
                             ▼
          Tax Rule & Exemption Engine
                             │
                             ▼
              Load into MySQL / Oracle
                             │
                             ▼
         Investor Statements & Tax Reports
                             │
                             ▼
          Logging, Monitoring & Notifications
Key Takeaways
•	Modular Pipeline Architecture improves maintainability, scalability, and ease of enhancement. 
•	Configuration-Driven Ingestion enables onboarding of new brokers without modifying application code. 
•	Parallel Ingestion using Multiprocessing significantly reduces processing time for large financial datasets. 
•	Error Handling and Retry Mechanisms improve resilience by recovering automatically from transient failures. 
•	Comprehensive Unit Testing ensures data quality and prevents incorrect capital gain calculations. 
•	Coding Standards, Logging, and Deployment Practices enhance readability, operational support, and production stability. 
This architecture represents the foundational design principles used in enterprise-grade capital gain processing systems, enabling stock exchanges and financial institutions to process millions of transactions reliably, accurately, and within strict overnight processing windows.



1. Single Processing Pipeline (Sequential)
Files are processed one after another.
# single_processing.py

import time

files = ["trade_day1.csv", "trade_day2.csv", "trade_day3.csv"]


def process_file(file):
    print(f"Reading {file}...")
    time.sleep(2)          # Simulate processing
    print(f"{file} Completed")


def main():
    start = time.time()

    for file in files:
        process_file(file)

    end = time.time()

    print("\nAll files processed")
    print(f"Time Taken : {round(end-start,2)} seconds")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
Output
Reading trade_day1.csv...
trade_day1.csv Completed

Reading trade_day2.csv...
trade_day2.csv Completed

Reading trade_day3.csv...
trade_day3.csv Completed

All files processed
Time Taken : 6.01 seconds
________________________________________
2. Multiprocessing Pipeline (Parallel)
Now each file is processed by a separate process.
# multiprocessing_pipeline.py

from multiprocessing import Pool
import time

files = ["trade_day1.csv", "trade_day2.csv", "trade_day3.csv"]


def process_file(file):
    print(f"Reading {file}...")
    time.sleep(2)
    print(f"{file} Completed")


def main():

    start = time.time()

    with Pool(processes=3) as pool:
        pool.map(process_file, files)

    end = time.time()

    print("\nAll files processed")
    print(f"Time Taken : {round(end-start,2)} seconds")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
Output
Reading trade_day1.csv...
Reading trade_day2.csv...
Reading trade_day3.csv...

trade_day1.csv Completed
trade_day2.csv Completed
trade_day3.csv Completed

All files processed
Time Taken : 2.05 seconds
________________________________________
